The first number in the output above gives the number of linear transformations
performed by the routine while attempting to place I into the desired position.
The second number tells which *note BasisElementLimit:
(Macaulay2Doc)BasisElementLimit, was used when computing the (partial) Groebner
basis. By default, noetherNormalization tries to use a partial Groebner basis.
It does this by sequentially computing a Groebner basis with the option *note
BasisElementLimit: (Macaulay2Doc)BasisElementLimit, set to predetermined
values. The default values come from the following
list:{5,20,40,60,80,infinity}. To set the values manually, use the option
LimitList:
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : R = QQ[x_1..x_4];                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : I = ideal(x_2^2+x_1*x_2+1, x_1*x_2*x_3*x_4+1);                         |
